WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:01.770
Film Courage: Can you take us through the
sponsorship process?

00:00:01.770 --> 00:00:03.120
Zeke Zelker, Writer/Director/Producer of the
transmedia project BILLBOARD: Sure.

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:04.790
Film Courage: I’m sure each one was different.

00:00:04.790 --> 00:00:07.040
You said you had 6 sponsors?

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:08.890
Zeke: No, 6 revenue streams.

00:00:08.890 --> 00:00:13.680
We have at least 9 sponsors.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:18.320
Okay so sponsorship is different than product
placement.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:24.750
Product placement you basically want to have
company X in your your film and so you have

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:35.200
to clear it to be able to have their IP, their
trademark, their logo in your film.

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:39.989
But if you’re good at it you can get a lot
of free stuff out of it.

00:00:39.989 --> 00:00:43.010
I’ve had directors that I’ve worked with
in the past challenge me.

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:44.010
“Can you get me this?

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:45.010
Can you get me that?”

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:51.030
And generally oftentimes I can get them crazy
stuff but it’s a matter of knowing how to

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:56.000
do it.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:02.000
You have to know what you’re asking for
and you need to give them a reason why you’re

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:06.710
asking for it and you need to make sure you
have your contracts buttoned up and everything

00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:08.009
else as well.

00:01:08.009 --> 00:01:13.219
Now in terms of sponsorship it’s a matter
of making sure that they know there’s going

00:01:13.219 --> 00:01:17.359
to be an audience for your project and so
that’s one reason why you build audience

00:01:17.359 --> 00:01:24.149
early on and with that being said you have
to make the sponsor as comfortable with the

00:01:24.149 --> 00:01:29.492
project as possible and be very transparent
about what you are doing.

00:01:29.492 --> 00:01:32.829
Brands do not like when you bash their brand
obviously.

00:01:32.829 --> 00:01:34.189
That’s not going to work.

00:01:34.189 --> 00:01:41.899
I mean we’ve even had brands say that they
have to be the protagonist not the antagonist.

00:01:41.899 --> 00:01:45.719
So it gets to be a little bit tricky on certain
things.

00:01:45.719 --> 00:01:51.289
But the sponsorship process is creating a
deck, you knowing where and how their identity

00:01:51.289 --> 00:01:55.701
is going to be seen what their activation
are going to be above and beyond just the

00:01:55.701 --> 00:01:58.140
picture itself in terms of are there going
to be live events?

00:01:58.140 --> 00:01:59.499
Are there going to be premieres?

00:01:59.499 --> 00:02:04.639
Is there going to be anywhere where they could
attach themselves to.

00:02:04.639 --> 00:02:10.970
You’ve got to provide all of these packages
for the sponsors and so it’s work.

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:21.239
It’s a lot of work to be able to get but
we were able to raise…oh gosh?

00:02:21.239 --> 00:02:24.540
About 20% of our budget from sponsorship.

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:30.030
Film Courage: Wow and how are you choosing
which sponsors to approach?

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:38.849
Zeke: Research and what I want I guess you
would say.

00:02:38.849 --> 00:02:45.810
For BILLBOARD I first went to location businesses
because we wanted to feel authentic and so

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:49.709
it only made sense for us to show off local
businesses.

00:02:49.709 --> 00:02:54.439
And then after that w green screened the billboards
in the film so we can actually change out

00:02:54.439 --> 00:02:55.439
them all.

00:02:55.439 --> 00:03:00.870
If we go to India we can actually change out
the billboards to Hindi, French the same sort

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:02.970
of thing.

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:09.280
Again just thinking like an entrepreneur,
thinking like a business person.

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:14.930
We did that so the brands could then see the
content so they would okay it.

00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:19.420
So that was another reason to, to try to mitigate
as much risk as possible.

00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:24.329
Film Courage: So you find some brands that
you think will be a good fit and you’re

00:03:24.329 --> 00:03:25.329
calling them?

00:03:25.329 --> 00:03:26.829
Are you sending an email?

00:03:26.829 --> 00:03:33.760
Zeke: I always call first and then it’s
tracking down the right person you need to

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:35.909
speaking to because every company is different.

00:03:35.909 --> 00:03:43.090
With some of them it’s a brand manager,
with some of them it’s the CMO, some of

00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:49.030
them they have a sponsorship coordinator,
some of them they are movie development people.

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:52.599
Some companies use an outside party.

00:03:52.599 --> 00:03:56.359
Like I think it’s Tier 3 and a bunch of
other ones that we use as a clearinghouse

00:03:56.359 --> 00:03:58.349
for things so you have to talk to them.

00:03:58.349 --> 00:04:05.120
It’s a lot of leg work but I’ve been doing
it for so many years that this one gentleman

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:10.532
that was a junior in college that wanted to
intern for me during the summer and I’m

00:04:10.532 --> 00:04:13.959
like this is what you’re going to be doing
and he’s like how am I going to do that?

00:04:13.959 --> 00:04:16.840
“I’m like I’m going to teach you and
I’ll tell you what I need to do.”

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:20.049
And he would come back to me with questions
all the time.

00:04:20.049 --> 00:04:26.290
And by the end of the summer he was hired
by any movie studio because you’re scrappy.

00:04:26.290 --> 00:04:29.800
Film Courage: Once you’ve already established
who the brand manager is maybe you’ve had

00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:34.800
a phone call or made this initial contact,
you are sending a pitch deck?

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:41.330
Zeke: You send a deck and then you try and
show how their brand is going to be seen with

00:04:41.330 --> 00:04:42.330
the content.

00:04:42.330 --> 00:04:46.470
Film Courage: And then how much time do you
give them to look it over?

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:47.569
What’s protocol?

00:04:47.569 --> 00:04:49.710
Zeke: A week and then you start badgering
them.

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:50.710
Film Courage: A week, okay.

00:04:50.710 --> 00:04:51.729
And then you start calling?

00:04:51.729 --> 00:04:54.550
Zeke: And then you start negotiating.

00:04:54.550 --> 00:05:00.560
Everything is so different.

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:04.389
I could have had some major brans on our billboards.

00:05:04.389 --> 00:05:07.530
They didn’t necessarily want to give me
money but they would have given it in trade

00:05:07.530 --> 00:05:12.240
out of exposure and I walked away from those
deals.

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:14.040
Film Courage: Why?

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:24.539
Zeke: Because I thought my project had more
value and I don’t know…in retrospect I

00:05:24.539 --> 00:05:29.270
wish I would have for two of them quite honestly.

00:05:29.270 --> 00:05:35.750
But I made the wrong decision straight up
I don’t know what else to say?

00:05:35.750 --> 00:05:39.760
Because I could have done some fun things
and at the same time it might have limited

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:47.800
us from other things but another thing was
did I want them to be a part of the project?

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:52.160
Deep down inside (what they’re selling)
did I want them to be a part of it?

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:56.520
And so that was kind of like a gut call that
I made.

00:05:56.520 --> 00:06:04.530
Film Courage: Right because that’s going
to live on in your film so you don’t feel

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:06.310
right about that, that makes sense.

00:06:06.310 --> 00:06:08.460
Zeke: I’m not telling you who it was.

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:11.350
Film Courage: No that’s okay, I wasn’t
going to ask.

00:06:11.350 --> 00:06:13.720
But now my mind is going to all different
places.

00:06:13.720 --> 00:06:18.280
Zeke: You see so you have to have integrity.

00:06:18.280 --> 00:06:22.819
Even though you are an entrepreneur think
about the business side of the film business

00:06:22.819 --> 00:06:24.390
you still have to have integrity and vision.

00:06:24.390 --> 00:06:32.940
And that’s the thing you can’t sell out
because if you sell out what are you going

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:34.009
to do next.

00:06:34.009 --> 00:06:37.150
Film Courage: That’s so…you know it’s
funny because I just watched this documentary

00:06:37.150 --> 00:06:44.440
on The Clash (it was on the day that Joe Strummer
passed away 16 years ago) and I was looking

00:06:44.440 --> 00:06:47.629
at all the comments and they were like “They
sold out!

00:06:47.629 --> 00:06:49.979
They’re champagne socialists, etc.”

00:06:49.979 --> 00:06:53.900
But who wouldn’t want to go to a higher
level?

00:06:53.900 --> 00:06:57.360
I get frustrated with the whole sell out notion.

00:06:57.360 --> 00:06:59.780
That’s a very “indie argument.”

00:06:59.780 --> 00:07:04.400
Because there’s commercial, there’s indie,
there’s the integrity of the creativity.

00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:06.629
Zeke: It’s funny because I don’t look
at it as selling out.

00:07:06.629 --> 00:07:15.560
I think it’s a different way of discovering
your pain as an artist.

00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:23.090
Become I think all artists are trying to overcome
some kind of pain so that they can be understood.

00:07:23.090 --> 00:07:24.090
I know I am.

00:07:24.090 --> 00:07:27.120
So I’m only really speaking to my own experience.

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:32.470
But you give The Clash as an example and I
wouldn’t say they sold out.

00:07:32.470 --> 00:07:37.240
I’d say they figured out a different way
to display their pain.

00:07:37.240 --> 00:07:45.610
They did have success (meteoric success mind
you) in a form that wasn’t even a form yet

00:07:45.610 --> 00:07:50.740
(a music form) . You could say the Sex Pistols
before them .

00:07:50.740 --> 00:07:56.610
Film Courage: Did they get as much radio play
as The Clash did?

00:07:56.610 --> 00:07:57.690
Zeke: No.

00:07:57.690 --> 00:07:58.690
No.

00:07:58.690 --> 00:07:59.690
No.

00:07:59.690 --> 00:08:03.310
I mean The Clash went international, not just
in the UK.

00:08:03.310 --> 00:08:05.500
But at the same time it I just think who doesn’t
like champagne?

00:08:05.500 --> 00:08:09.830
Film Courage: You don’t have to be a socialist
to like champagne.

00:08:09.830 --> 00:08:13.780
I’m just repeating what a commenter wrote
underneath the video.

00:08:13.780 --> 00:08:16.060
Those are someone else’s words not mine.

00:08:16.060 --> 00:08:21.370
Mind you I don’t think they sold out but
it’s just so fascinating that whole argument.

00:08:21.370 --> 00:08:29.130
And I know in certain genres that’s a very
touchy subject (selling out) whether it’s

00:08:29.130 --> 00:08:31.069
punk rock or whatever style.

00:08:31.069 --> 00:08:37.000
Zeke: Well you know it’s funny because as
a punker we had those people who we would

00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:38.880
call posers.

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:42.170
Those people who thought they were and wanted
to be but weren’t true.

00:08:42.170 --> 00:08:45.020
Film Courage: I’ve heard the term before!

00:08:45.020 --> 00:08:51.900
Zeke: I’ve got friends who are artists and
actors and musicians who feel like they sold

00:08:51.900 --> 00:08:52.900
out.

00:08:52.900 --> 00:08:54.350
And I’m like what the heck does that mean?

00:08:54.350 --> 00:08:58.100
That they actually had some commercial success
and you don’t and you’re jealous?

